course: dictionary
lesson: 9351
title: Splendid and its shades of meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Splendid» has two main meanings. The first is very beautiful, grand and impressive, as in «a splendid palace». The second, a little old-fashioned in British English, is very good or excellent, as in «What a splendid idea!»
explain: Context tells you which meaning you need. A palace is a building you can look at, so splendid means grand and impressive. An idea cannot be looked at, so splendid there means excellent. Ask yourself: is this thing beautiful to see, or is it simply very good?
explain: Splendid also has an adverb form, splendidly, which describes how something is done. Splendid can be compared too: more splendid, most splendid.
explain: Think about who is speaking. In The Wind in the Willows, Toad uses «splendid» about himself, his house and his motor-cars. «Over lunch Toad tells all his adventures as if they were splendid fun, and the Rat grows more and more grave and silent.» The Rat's silence is a clue: Toad is excited, but his friend is not impressed.
fact: Splendid is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as a splendid palace or a splendid idea.
fact: «Splendid» means very beautiful, grand and impressive.
fact: In British English, especially older-style speech, «splendid» means very good or excellent.
fact: The adverb «splendidly» means in a splendid way, as in «performed splendidly».
fact: The word sounds a little old-fashioned, so you will often meet it in classic books and in British speech.
exercise: «We stood in awe of the splendid palace.» What does splendid mean here? | *Very beautiful, grand and impressive | Very noisy and busy | Very small and plain | Very cold and empty
exercise: «What a splendid idea!» Which meaning fits best? | Very beautiful and grand | Very old and dusty | *Very good; excellent | Very late in the day
exercise: Toad says, «My motor-car is splendid!» What does Toad probably mean? | *He thinks his car is excellent and impressive | He is sorry the car is broken | He is describing a palace | He wants to sell the car cheaply
test: Which word is the adverb form of splendid? | splendour | splendid | *splendidly
test: In old-style British speech, «Splendid!» means: | *Great! | Cold! | Quiet! | Terrible!
exam: Toad calls his house «splendid», but the Rat grows grave and silent. Which meaning of splendid fits Toad's use? | *Very beautiful, grand and impressive | Very hungry | Very short and rude | Very cold and quiet
